Yeah. After perhaps six weeks of intermittant
trying, suddenly the Cygwin setup.exe started
working for me, after it had been freezing up in
dozens of prior tries, and I finally have a full set
of Unix work-alike commands, and a decent command
line entry window (rxvt). Yes!

Cygwin makes MS-Windows pretty much something you
can ignore as a behind the scenes bit of bad news,
and instead do software development as civilized
people do, with GNU toolkit tools.

It comes by default with Python, Ruby, gcc (C, C++),
g77 (FORTRAN 77), an ideosyncratic Java, PERL,
bash, ash (minimal shell), gawk GNU AWK), sed (and
probably more) for programming languages. Many more
can be download separately.

Recommended.

